[
  "Welcome back to The Sound Above. I am Mike Hall. Today we enter The AI Horizon & The Observable Control Plane. Before code synthesis models and automated agents, developers met face to face to establish standards of craftsmanship. Today we rewatch Episode 31: Robert \"Uncle Bob\" Martin from SCNA 2012 (2012).",
  "Recorded at SCNA 2012 in Chicago while Uncle Bob was building the Clean Coders video series.",
  "What does a craftsman owe an audience? How did the author of Clean Code think about teaching, performance, and professionalism? What principles guided Robert \"Uncle Bob\" Martin when this conversation was recorded? How do those lessons inform the software systems we design today?",
  "Roll archive footage (YouTube ID: 5rardEzrQOk). Watch for the unvarnished hallway exchanges, the emphasis on craft over speed, and the sheer enthusiasm of engineers taking responsibility for their software.",
  "When a model can emit code instantly, professionalism shifts to what we refuse to ship. The standards he taught are the review bar for generated code. When implementation is cheap, verification and clarity become everything. We cannot accept the mirage of competence. Let us examine the terminal contracts directly.",
  "Thank you for joining today's session. Full transcripts, provenance notes, and companion research are available at just3ws.com. In our next broadcast, we explore Episode 32. Until next time, keep your feedback loops fast and your code humane."
]